Date: Fri, 28 May 93 11:00:46 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: ECON: Ignorance on Homesteading vs. Auctioning

That is, *my* ignorance on homesteading vs. auctioning.

I haven't put my two cents in on this controversy because I have no
real knowledge. But I do find this an intensely Extropian topic!

- frontiers, Schelling points, auctions, world government,
homesteading the Amazon jungle, ownership of new discoveries, etc.,
are all involved, for better or for worse.

- and many real-world issues: spectrum allocation, patents and
discoveries, and market incentives.


If it matters, I'm leaning toward Tim Starr's horror at the idea of
setting up a new level of bureacracy to deal with the oceans, the
skies, the Moon, etc., and then to hold these things as the "common
heritage of Mankind," or somesuch. (My guess is that a U.N. or World
Government which is given title/seizes title/whatever, will never in
fact auction off even a single piece...the pressure to "husband" these
"rights" through permits, rent, etc., will be too great.)

I could see the same general idea extended to intellectual property,
with all discoveries being held by the U.N. for the "common heritage"
of Mankind. A billion Chinese,  a bunch of Indians, most of Africa,
would like nothing more than to gain control of the future of
technology--not to invent it, but to collect their "fair share." (I'll
stop ranting on this now....I'm betraying my objectivity and also
sounding like a certain Polish mathematician!)

Some cases which might be looked at in more detail:

- allocation of the broadcast spectrum. Was it "homesteading" or was
it "auctioning," and how did the outcome compare to what might have
happened differently? (It's not just the FCC, it's also "WARC," the
world  body/committeee that assigns channels and bands to nations, to
the Third World (for future use), etc. I'd be very curious to know
exactly which model they use (probably a mixture) and how the analysis
of homesteading vs. auctioning vs. natural Schelling points would
apply. For example, would broadcasters find "Schelling frequencies" on
their own, or would they constantly encroach on and interfere with
neighbors? (My extremely speculative hunch: in a "spectral anarchy,"
the hassles of interference and claim-jumping would have resulted by
now in a better technological solution: the transition to fiber optics
and other narrowcasting strategies which allow huge increases in
channel capacity and, more importantly, the creation of "proprietary
spaces" (this is the main bandwidth expander: Shannon's channel
capacity applies separately to each fiber or narrowcasted transmission).)

I mention this because this thread has direct relevance to _today's_
problems, not just to colonizing physical space.

- what policies are in effect in the Brazilian rainforests? (My
impression is that "homesteading" is having the deleterious effect of
encouraging Brazilian peasants to "homestead" a piece of land, burn
off the native rainforest vegetation, plant one or two crops, exhaust
the thin soil, and then move on to a new homestead...if so, this is a
pattern much different than seen in the American experience, where the
land had a continuing future value.)

- how does this analysis apply to patents and intellectual property in
general? One might imagine all discoveries held in a "common reserve"
by the nations of the Earth (Robin's scenario) and then auctioned off,
whereas we currently grant/respect "homesteading" rights to the first
discoverer. My hunch is that a Hanson-type system applied to patents
and discoveries--even beyond the current system, which has some
elements of it (mandatory patent licensing, for example)--would
concentrate incredible power in the government...and inventors would
then be encouraged to be very secretive, to not publicize their
inventions, to keep them as trade secrets. Of course, the government
might then require audits of lab notebooks, internal memos, etc., to
make sure the "common heritage" is being reported to the IRS
(Invention Reporting Service). I'm getting sarcastic here, so I'll stop.

Whatever our views at this time, this is a very timely issue. In fact,
I think this would make a great topic for a comprehensive study, even
a dissertation! (Robin, I vote you study this at Caltech next year!)

If the Extropy Institute wants to someday have an influence remotely
similar to the Cato Institute, this sort of study could fit right in.

Very exciting! 


-Tim May

