Date: Fri, 28 May 93 11:23:59 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON/SPACE: Sell the Universe Whole

Hal Finney writes:
>First, it seems wiser to auction off things a bit at a time than all at
>once.  First do near-earth orbital space.  Then do the moon when we are
>ready.  Then mars or the asteroids.  The experience gained at earlier levels
>will make the value estimates more accurate for alter auctions.

Yes, my latest proposal allows exactly this scenario.

>Second, an alternative to the auction is what Tim talked about, where
>everybody is just given some ownership of the off-planet resources.  Then
>organizations would go around and buy up people's shares in order to put
>together the deals they want.  I don't know if this has any different formal
>results than an auction where the proceeds are delivered equally to everybody
>on earth.

I'd say it's rather different.  Tremendous transaction costs are
incurred in mapping out all the different plots, randomly allocating
them, distributing these assets.  Worse great negotiation costs would
be incurred rebundling these assets if this was a poor way to divide
them.  Even Dani's suggested alternative is almost as bad.  

Mike Price writes:
>To wit, the Aussie settlers were able
>to lose themselves in the outback and establish settlements without
>interference until it was too late.  Sounds like a similar thing
>happened in some parts of the US when they tried auctioning. ...
>I claim it's not feasible due to the technologies and geometry of space. 
>In the early days the ratio of explored to unexplored space within a few
>light-decades is to low to allow regular patrolling.  

Well what I had most in mind was the settling of the inner solar
system, and the technology of war and detection there seems much more
supportive of enforcement.  I think it will be easy to see all the
settlers from here, and send a missle they couldn't easily defend
against or even detect until it almost got to them.  Military
equilibrium seems much more like MAD with nukes today.  

James A. Donald writes:

>The problem [with auctions] is that they require a power with capacity
>to coerce, thus they require that the resource is already owned by
>someone with the capacity to defend it.  In the case of space, such a
>capacity would be dangerously great.  
>This point has already been made repeatedly.  I would rather not "hear
>more specific reasons on these points."

Words have been repeated, but point not made.  I have said explicitly
that I do not propose creating special enforcment, and fully expect
many violations more in the homesteading direction.  The enforcement
is through the Schelling point power of a treaty, coordinating private
uncommunicatable expectations about who will defend what how much.
And any degree of reduction of full homesteading is desireable, as it
increases the value of the universe to us.  

I agree with Krzys' that nations effectively own a lot now, and moral
indignation doesn't make that go away.  Best to buy them off.  There
is a useful analogy with post-communist privatization, in that
political fighting over how things should be privatized threatens to
eat up much of the value of what they are fighting over.   

I'm not sure Tim Starr is really in the mood for a detached evaluation
of homesteading.  He uses a lot of emotional language.  Tim wants to
call any creation of property rights "homesteading", any alternative
to "pioneers take first posession" is "interventionist", and any
treaty is "coersive". 

Tim also gives what I think are not a few misrepresentations.  I have
not proposed trusting the United Nations to manage the universe, nor
that the UN "threaten to back this claim up with force", nor argued
"that homesteading is a market failure and that it's possible for
government to solve it".  I am really not interested in conversing at
the ideological level.

I can respond to concrete concerns.  Tim wonders "Why would the owner
sell anything?  Why not simply collect monopoly rents from the rest of
us?"  The answer is that the treaty forbids them to sell anything
before it can be used commercially.  If they violate the treaty, their
claims are void, and worthless.

Robin Hanson

