Date: Fri, 28 May 93 13:59:33 -0700
From: davisd@pierce.ee.washington.edu
Subject: ECON/SPACE: Sell the Universe Whole 

  To compare auctions and homesteading, may I please ask both sides'
  comments on the problem of privatization in post-communist economies:
  Auction -- the government owns everything, so it can auction it
  Homesteading -- the citizens have slaved away for the masters for long
  enough, now they have an ownership claim based on homesteading, 
  distribute the property in shares proportional to actuarial accumulated 
  value of what's due them.

  Krzys'

The key point to these auctions, in my mind, is that the proceeds have
to be divided up among all those who are considered to have an equal right 
to the property.

I agree that the (former) soviet citizens who have slaved away this
century have a right to whatever property was produced. I don't think
homesteading works because it is not at all clear what size of
"plot" each citizen has been working, nor which pieces of property
should go to who.

The only way decent way to get the property into private hands 
is to auction it off and give the proceeds to the citizens, or give
all the citizens 1000 credits, and then auction off the property
to the highest bidder in credits, allowing a free market in credits.

The latter might be better, since it allows participation by the 
citizens before they've received any of the proceeds of the auction.

There has been a lot of complaints that an auction can only be held
if we acknowledge an owner. Hogwash. The auctioneer can simply be
an institution which mediates the competing claims to the property
by auction. As long as the participants in the auction respect
the method of mediation, no one needs to acknowledge the mediator
as a prior owner of anything.

Buy Buy -- Dan Davis

