From: pcm@cs.brown.edu (Peter C. McCluskey)
Subject: New Brain Test: "Einstein einstein.... Penrose!"
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute freely

 jamesdon@infoserv.com ("James A. Donald") writes:
>The results of Keith M. Kendrick New Scientist 12 May 1990 Vol 126 No 1716
>p62-65 and Keith M. Kendrick Phys. Abs. 7-133282, Science USA Vol 236 no 4800
>p448-50 on visual perception seem to put a serious strain on the hypothesis
>that individual neurons do only simple things.

 Having checked the first of those references, I see no indication of such a
strain.

>Current computers cannot extract high level categories from low level
>features at all, even in an enormous number of steps.  In fact it requires
>a non polynomially large number of steps for a computer to do what either
>individual nerve cells are doing in this case, or a small number of nerve 
>cells are doing in this case.

 Current computers do some high-level categorization, but do not come close
to matching biological systems. They also do not come close to matching
the processing power of biological systems, so we can't know how far
the algorithms themselves are from matching biological behavior.
 The term "non polynomially large number of steps" is meaningless without
reference to a variable that you are scaling up. Whatever you have in mind,
I doubt that anyone knows what the lower bound is.

>Furthermore, if neurons do only simple things, a bee should not have 
>sufficient brain power to recognize categories

 Why do you expect us to believe this?

>Neural networks supposedly modeled on natural neural networks conspicuously
>fail to display perceptual invariance, which is a major and obvious feature
>of the capabilities of most simple animals, and the major design goal of
>most neural network designs.

 If by perceptual invariance you mean the ability to recognize objects at
independent of position, orientation, size, and color, then neural networks
have been fairly successful. I refer you to:

 Michael Seibert and Allen Waxman, "Learning Aspect Graph Representations
from View Sequences" in Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 2,
Morgan Kaufman 1990.

 Shelly D.D. Goggin, Kristina M. Johnson, and Karl E. Gustafson,
 "A Second-Order Translation, Rotation, and Scale Invariant Neural Network"
in Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 3, Morgan Kaufman 1991.

 Spirkovska, Lilly and Max B. Reid, "Robust Position, Scale and Rotation
Invariant Object Recognition using Higher-Order Neural Networks", Pattern
Recognition 25, pp 975-985 (1992).

 Kunihiko Fukushima, Sei Miyake, and Takayuki Ito, "Neocognitron: a neural
network model for a mechanism of visual pattern recognition", IEEE
Transactions on Systems, Man, and Cybernetics vol 13, p 826-834 (1983).

