Subject: Article: 'Get the Amateurs Out of High Tech'

There's an article in the June '93 issue of _Upside_ magazine:

"Get the Amateurs Out of High Tech" 
by T.J. Rodgers, CEO of Cypress Semiconductor Corp.

Facing the title page is a cartoon of little-boy versions of
Clinton and Gore building a tower of pc boards and chips,
spilling out of a box labeled "Build Your Own HI-TECH
Policy...Ages 6-46".  Good laissez-faire rant.

Starts out: "Cypress Semiconductor Corp. would benefit greatly if
billions of taxpayer dollars were showered on the technology 
projects favored by the Clinton administration.  It would be easy
for me to support these projects.  But such subsidies will hurt
my company and our industry."

Against tax-and-spend, says it's a misconception that high-tech
leaders are behind Clinton, says rather than tax and invest in
"data superhighways" that are already being built, get 
telecommunications regulations out of the way, cut govt. spending 
drastically, and (I found this a little strange) restructure the 
capital-gains tax (rather than remove or reduce it) in order not 
to look like it's giving the rich a break.

Upside prints articles like this regularly.  Good, I guess, if
you want to impress someone who's impressed by glossy magazines.

That issue also features an article by Steve Ditlea about/
interview with Jaron Lanier and about the collapse/diaspora of VPL.

