Subject: ECON/SPACE: Sell the Universe Whole

I went to a session of the International Space Development Conference
this afternoon which was a presentation on property rights in space.

The NSS is starting to agitate now for changes in the Moon Treaty,
which comes up for review in the UN in 1994. The US is _not_ a
signatory, but such notable space powers as Chile and Uruguay are. If
we can get the "common heritage of mankind" language changed in favor
of something that allows homesteading and rational exploitation, then
there may be a chance to start a lunar land rush _without_ explicit
national sovereignty, since the 1967 space treaty, which the US & USSR
_did_ ratify, keeps nations per se from claiming celestial bodies.

This sounded very promising, and I was glad to hear NSS types talking
the talk -- hell, the presenter even used the term "statist"! Of
course, there's a fairly big hole in this -- the naive assumption that
the states in the UN, or the US itself, can be held to their own rules
when profits are being made.

Dani Eder is giving a talk on Monday, which I will probably miss, as I've
been away from my office too long between Windows World/Comdex and the
ISDC. He should look me up already.

Also, Drexler should be arriving sometime Saturday, as he is presenting
on Saturday afternoon and Sunday morning.
