Date: Fri, 28 May 93 22:59:55 CDT
From: ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu
Subject: ECON/IDEAS: Auction All Intellectual Property!

Actually, I have been discussing versions of this idea in my patents and
trade secrets course for the last year or two. One problem with the present
system for allocating intellectual property is that it can lead to
inefficient patent races, since the person who makes the invention (or, in
other countries, files) a day earlier gets the whole pie. I have a
published article (with Landes and Posner) in which I argue that one
respect in which trade secret is superior to patent is that, if the cost of
making a particular invention is falling rapidly with time, that reduces
the social value of inventing it now (since you can invent it much more
cheaply next year), it reduces the value of inventing it and keeping it a
trade secret now, but does not reduce the value of inventing and patenting
it now. So in this respect, trade secret gives a better pattern of
incentives than patent.

In the literature, there is something called the "prospect theory" of
intellectual property (due to Ed Kitsch), which argues that the real
function of a patent is not, or not mainly, rewarding innovation but rather
establishing property rights in a range of intellectual property (like the
property rights you get by a mineral claim), in order that the owner can
then exploit the property efficiently. One implication is that patenting
something should give you control over a considerable area, in order that
you can then prevent inefficient patent races within that area. The problem
with this is that a broad patent is worth more, so this rule gives you a
bigger dissipation of resources in the race for the initial patent.

The proposal I have made in class is that we solve this problem by
auctioning off patents for things before they are invented. In my version,
someone goes to the patent office with a proposed patent defining a region
of intellectual property. If the patent office agrees that it is a sensible
bundle, it puts it up for auction. The only advantage that the person who
starts the process has (and his incentive for going to the patent office in
the first place) is that he has designed the bundle--presumably to fit what
he thinks he is in a particularly good position to invent.

Note that this does not eliminate the incentive to invent things--the
auction price will be the value of the patent minus the estimated cost of
making the invention (to the second lowest cost inventor--assuming no
strategic behavior or collusion). It simply moves the property right, and
the associated coordination responsibility, into private hands.

I am not, by the way, arguing that this is a desirable proposal--although I
think it is a useful way of pointing out certain features of the logic of
the problem of intellectual property. I am actually pretty agnostic about
what the best intellectual property regime is, there being fairly good
arguments for a range of different ones, all the way from no protection to
protection much broader than we now allow. It probably would be a desirable
proposal in a world where it was obvious what the right bundles of property
rights were--where, in other words, the normal situation was "seven firms
are trying to invent an X, and the only question is who will get it first."
It is much less desirable in a world where it is very unclear what sorts of
things can be invented, and where waiting until they are invented therefore
gives you information about how the intellectual territory ought to be
bundled. It is still less desirable in a world where the main part of an
invention is thinking of the idea, so that someone who tells the patent
office what he wants them to auction has, by doing so, just given away his
valuable invention. 

David Friedman
University of Chicago Law School


