Federal Technology Report 

April  29, 1993

SECTION: Pg. 10

LENGTH: 554 words

HEADLINE: GOVERNMENT PICKS TWO FIRMS TO MARKET 
ENCRYPTION DEVICE 

BODY:

President Clinton has announced development of a new encryption 
technology that government officials claim will improve the security 
and privacy of telephone communications while also allowing 
wiretapping by authorized law enforcement agencies. 

A new microcircuit, called the " Clipper Chip, " will permit law 
enforcement personnel to unscramble encrypted information by 
accessing each device's unique serial numbers or "keys." 

Two such keys for each device are to be deposited separately in 
"key-escrow" databases that will be established by the attorney 
general. Law enforcement agencies would need to obtain 
wiretapping authority, in most cases a court order, to obtain access 
to the keys. 

The technology was developed by the super secret National Security 
Agency, which has licensed two California firms to produce the 
chips. According to Raymond Kammer, acting director of the 
National Institute of Standards and Technology, Mycotronx, 
Torrance, Calif., designed the chips. The firm now is programming 
the chips, which are produced at a foundry owned by VLSI Inc. 
VLSI also incorporates a feature designed to prevent reverse 
engineering.

Sole-source Decision 

Kammer said the firms were selected on a sole-source basis because 
they are believed to be the only two in the nation capable of 
producing the devices. But he said the government recognizes it will 
need to seek and qualify other vendors to produce the chips. 

Kammer said the federal government intends to purchase the chips 
and use them for its own unclassified but sensitive communication 
needs. He said that when purchased in lots of 10,000, the chips cost 
about $ 25 apiece. The initial purchase of "several thousand" of the 
devices will be made by the Department of Justice for use in secure 
law enforcement communications, the White House said. 

Kammer said the government hopes state and local governments, as 
well as the private sector, will also adopt the  Clipper Chip  
technology, as they have done with its predecessor standard, the 15-
year-old Data Encryption Standard (DES). 

Kammer stressed that the government had no way to force others to 
adopt the new technology, but said the private sector should be 
attracted by  Clipper 

Chip's  advanced technology. By way of comparison, he said, while 
it would require a Cray Y-MP supercomputer 200 years to exhaust 
all possible combinations in DES, a billion years would be needed to 
do the same with the  Clipper  Chip.  

NIST has responsibility for developing security standards to protect 
unclassified federal computer systems. 

Businesses have become increasingly aware of the need for cost-
effective protection of information such as trade secrets and bid 
information. At the same time, terrorists, drug dealers and 
organized crime have recognized the advantages of encryption to 
conceal their illegal enterprises from law enforcement. 

Kammer insisted that the new chip offers "no trap door," or method 
for government entities to surreptitiously access the 
communications. But he declined to say whether NSA, which is 
forbidden by law from intercepting domestic communications, 
could break  Clipper Chip -encoded data. 

At least one group, the Electronic Frontier Foundation Inc., raised 
the trap door issue in calling on the government to make the chip's 
algorithm public. 


